# ─── tbox-finder .gitattributes ────────────────────────────────────────────
# Line-ending normalization: the laptop, the amlab cluster, and CI must all see
# byte-identical LF text, so golden-hash regression (CLAUDE.md §8.2/§8.4) is
# reproducible across machines.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Byte-exact test inputs: never normalize (hash-stability for golden fixtures).
tests/fixtures/** -text
tests/golden/**   -text

# ─── git-LFS-tracked medium binaries (CLAUDE.md §5.2) ───────────────────────
# Large FASTAs / genomes live under the gitignored data/raw|external and go to
# DVC — only small committed benchmark FASTAs, covariance models and figures are
# LFS. The split-assignment table is deliberately NOT (see its stanza below).
figures/** filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.cm filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.fa filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.fasta filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
# The curated-control query FASTA LEFT git-LFS for the same reason as the split
# table below: tests/unit/test_curated_control_run.py reads it as REAL sequences
# through mining/curated_control_run.py, whose producer refuses a pointer outright
# ("is a git-LFS POINTER, not sequences"), so a pointer checkout turns 5 tests red.
# 61 KB / 317 records is far below what LFS is for. The explicit -filter/-diff/-merge
# are required to override the `*.fasta` rule immediately above; `-text` keeps the
# bytes byte-identical so the committed staging digest still matches.
data/processed/mining/curated_control_queries_v0.fasta -filter -diff -merge -text
# The split-assignment table LEFT git-LFS (was the PRD §9.2 carve-out). It is the
# ONE committed binary that CI must read as real bytes — the §8.2 no-leakage gate
# reads the real partition — so under LFS every `test` job spent bandwidth on it,
# and the blanket `lfs: true` it forced dragged all 31 objects (19.7 MB/run,
# ~11 GB/month) until the account's LFS budget was exhausted on 2026-08-24 and CI
# stopped being able to fetch anything at all. At 5.1 MB with 4 revisions in the
# repo's whole history it sits well inside plain git (GitHub warns at 50 MB), so
# as an ordinary blob it costs zero metered bandwidth, forever. Bonus: the amlab
# cluster has no git-lfs binary, so this file used to arrive there as a 132-byte
# pointer — the thing slurm/p2/*.sbatch carry explicit guards against.
# `-text` is load-bearing: `* text=auto eol=lf` above must never touch a parquet.
data/processed/splits/split_assignments.parquet -text
# Rendered per-phase dev-log PDFs (CLAUDE.md §6.2/§11) accumulate one ~1 MB
# binary per phase — LFS keeps the git packfile lean, like figures above.
docs/dev-log/*.pdf filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text

# Empty .gitkeep placeholders must stay plain text, never LFS pointers.
**/.gitkeep -filter -diff -merge text
